{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789913713,"nonce":"n52TtaxLKoDrklKBVpxuwhw0J/0=","title":"Lua: Number as an unboxed Int|Float","message":"`Ty::join(Int, Float)` is Any, a heap box per value. collatz's `x = x / 2` else `x = 3 * x + 1` makes x Any, and the kernel allocates at 2 GB/s (339 collections) for integer arithmetic.\n\nAdd `Ty::Number`: the join of Int and Float, lowered as a two-word value struct {tag, bits} that lives in registers. Arithmetic on it branches on the tag: both Int takes the integer path (wrapping, and `/` always to float), otherwise both convert to f64. Comparisons and equality follow Lua's exact int/float rules. Boxing happens only where a Number meets Any (a table store, a call into the library), by tag. join(Number, Any) stays Any.","files":null}]}